+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — hifitime 4.3.0
+
+Property-based tests were added with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest` dev-dependency)
+to the existing integration test files `tests/duration.rs` and `tests/epoch.rs`
+(modules `hegel_props`). 17 hegel tests cover 13 distinct properties. Four tests
+are **deliberately failing**: they pin real bugs with deterministic counterexamples
+(see *Suspected bugs*). Each pinned bug has a green "sibling" property that excludes
+only the known-buggy region so the surrounding behavior stays under test.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+The crate ships extensive kani proof harnesses (`src/**/kani_verif.rs`), but those
+are almost entirely no-panic checks with structural postconditions (results are
+normalized, saturate at MIN/MAX, time scale matches). The properties below target
+what kani does not: value-level semantics, round-trips, and oracle agreement.
+
+### Duration (`tests/duration.rs::hegel_props`)
+
+1. **`prop_duration_total_nanoseconds_roundtrip`** — `Duration::from_total_nanoseconds`
+   is the inverse of `total_nanoseconds` for in-range i128 values and clamps to
+   MIN/MAX outside. Evidence: rustdoc of both functions; kani proves normalization
+   but not value fidelity. Full-range i128 generation plus boundary boosts.
+2. **`prop_duration_add_matches_i128_oracle`** — `a + b` equals saturating i128
+   addition of total nanoseconds, clamped to the representable range. Evidence:
+   `Add` docs, `test_bounds`/`test_ops_near_bounds` saturation examples.
+3. **`prop_duration_sub_matches_i128_oracle`** — same for subtraction.
+   **FAILING — pins bug #1** (wrong saturation direction).
+   Sibling **`prop_duration_sub_matches_i128_oracle_no_century_overflow`** (green)
+   constructs pairs whose century difference fits in i16.
+4. **`prop_duration_neg_matches_i128_oracle`** — `-d` equals i128 negation (the
+   range is symmetric, `-MIN == MAX` per the `Neg` impl), hence an involution.
+5. **`prop_duration_ordering_matches_total_nanoseconds`** — the derived
+   lexicographic `Ord` on `(centuries, nanoseconds)` orders durations by value.
+   Evidence: the representation convention documented on the `Duration` struct.
+6. **`prop_duration_display_fromstr_roundtrip`** — `Display` → `FromStr` is
+   lossless. Evidence: the serde `Serialize`/`Deserialize` impls literally use
+   `to_string`/`from_str`, so serde round-trip integrity requires this.
+   **FAILING — pins bug #2** (f64 error in `decompose()` corrupts `Display`).
+   Sibling **`prop_duration_display_fromstr_roundtrip_subday`** (green) restricts
+   to sub-day durations where the f64 component math is exact.
+7. **`prop_duration_from_str_never_panics`** — parse robustness on arbitrary
+   Unicode text and duration-shaped strings. Green (the duration parser slices
+   only at char boundaries / on raw bytes).
+
+### Epoch (`tests/epoch.rs::hegel_props`)
+
+8. **`prop_epoch_timescale_roundtrip_non_utc`** — for all 78 ordered pairs of the
+   12 non-UTC time scales, `e.to_time_scale(to).to_time_scale(from) == e` over
+   ±292 years (i64 nanoseconds), **exactly** for fixed-offset scales
+   (TAI/TT/GPST/GST/BDT/QZSST), within 2 ns for rate-scaled scales (TCG/TL/TCL)
+   and 100 ns for iterated dynamical scales (ET/TDB/TCB). Evidence:
+   `test_timescale_recip`, `formal_epoch_reciprocity_tdb`, `sofa_val_tcg` (≤1 ns),
+   `tcl_tl_round_trip` (≤1 ns). Green at 10× case count.
+9. **`prop_epoch_utc_roundtrip`** — round-trip through UTC from fixed-offset
+   scales, generation boosted to within ±2 min of all 28 IERS leap-second
+   boundaries. **FAILING — pins bug #3** (1 s error in a ΔAT-wide window around
+   every leap boundary). Sibling
+   **`prop_epoch_utc_roundtrip_away_from_leap_boundaries`** (green) tests both
+   directions (X→UTC→X and UTC→X→UTC) for epochs >60 s from every boundary,
+   asserting **exact** round-trips.
+10. **`prop_epoch_gregorian_roundtrip`** — `to_gregorian(ts)` followed by
+    `maybe_from_gregorian(..., ts)` returns the exact same epoch, for all 13 time
+    scales over the (almost) full ±32,767-century Duration range. Both directions
+    are pure integer calendar math. Evidence: `extreme_gregorian_print_roundtrip`
+    does this for hand-picked dates from year −200,000 to +200,000. Green,
+    including at 10×.
+11. **`prop_epoch_from_str_never_panics`** — parse robustness for
+    `Epoch::from_str`. **FAILING — pins bug #4** (byte-index slicing panic on
+    multi-byte UTF-8). Sibling **`prop_epoch_from_str_never_panics_ascii`**
+    (green) fuzzes the parser with ASCII-only garbage and format-shaped strings.
+12. **`prop_epoch_display_fromstr_roundtrip`** — `Display` → `from_str` is exact
+    for all time scales and all valid Gregorian dates in years 1–9999 with
+    nanosecond precision. Evidence: serde impls for Epoch, `tcl_tl_round_trip`,
+    `test_timescale_recip` RFC3339 leg. Green at 10×.
+13. **`prop_epoch_time_of_week_roundtrip`** — `from_time_of_week ∘ to_time_of_week`
+    is the identity for non-negative epoch durations in every time scale (the
+    week counter is unsigned). Evidence: `test_time_of_week`; kani harnesses only
+    prove no-panic. Green at 10×.
+
+## Test results
+
+- `cargo test` (full suite): **all pre-existing tests pass** (including the 42
+  doc tests). Hegel tests: **13 of 17 pass**, 4 fail deliberately (bug pins).
+- Liveness check performed: temporarily inverting the ordering assertion in
+  property 5 produced a shrunk minimal counterexample
+  (`(0,0)` vs `(0,1)` — 0 ns vs 1 ns), then was reverted.
+- Exploratory 10× run (`test_cases = 1000`) performed on every property: no
+  additional failures beyond the four pinned bugs; reverted to defaults.
+- The four pinned tests fail deterministically: each embeds its counterexample
+  in a boosted `one_of!`/`just` branch and was verified to fail on 3 consecutive
+  fresh runs (with the `.hegel` failure database deleted between runs).
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+All four were found by the properties above, minimized by hegel's shrinker, and
+confirmed with standalone reproductions. None were fixed (per instructions).
+
+1. **`Duration::Sub` saturates to the wrong end on positive century overflow**
+   (`src/duration/ops.rs`, `Sub::sub`): when `a.centuries - b.centuries`
+   overflows i16 *positively* (large positive minus large negative), the
+   `checked_sub` `None` arm unconditionally returns `Duration::MIN`.
+   Counterexample: `Duration::ZERO - Duration::MIN` returns `Duration::MIN`
+   (−1,196,851,200 days) instead of `Duration::MAX`. The result has the wrong
+   sign by ~2.3 million years. Pinned in `prop_duration_sub_matches_i128_oracle`.
+
+2. **`Duration::decompose()` f64 rounding corrupts `Display` (and therefore
+   serde serialization)** (`src/duration/mod.rs`): components are computed with
+   `to_unit(...).floor()` in f64. From ~105 days upward, a duration just below a
+   day boundary rounds *up*, producing an inconsistent decomposition.
+   Counterexample: one century − 1 ns displays as
+   `"36525 days 59 min 59 s 999 ms 999 μs 999 ns"` (note: 36525 days is the full
+   century and the 23 h component is missing entirely); `decompose()` returns
+   `(0, 36525, 0, 59, 59, 999, 999, 999)`. Parsing the display back yields a
+   value ~1 h too large, so a serde round-trip silently corrupts the value.
+   Also reproduces at e.g. 250 days − 1 ns. Pinned in
+   `prop_duration_display_fromstr_roundtrip`.
+
+3. **TAI↔UTC conversions are inconsistent in a ΔAT-wide window at every leap
+   second boundary** (`src/epoch/mod.rs`, `to_time_scale`): the leap-second
+   table timestamps (`src/epoch/leap_seconds.rs`) are UTC calendar counts
+   ("assume this is TAI" convention — 2_272_060_800 s is 26,297 days, the UTC
+   count of 1972-01-01), and the UTC→TAI direction keys its lookup in that same
+   convention, but the TAI→UTC direction keys the lookup with the *true* TAI
+   value. Result: `TAI→UTC→TAI` is off by 1 s for every TAI epoch in
+   `[L, L + ΔAT_new)` — a 10–37 s window, e.g. 22 s wide at the 1983-07-01
+   boundary — and `UTC→TAI→UTC` is off by 1 s in `[L − ΔAT_old, L)`. Only a 1 s
+   window (the actual 23:59:60 leap second) is genuinely ambiguous; the rest of
+   the window is a keying inconsistency. Worst case pinned:
+   TAI 1972-01-01T00:00:00 → UTC 1971-12-31T23:59:50 → TAI 1971-12-31T23:59:50
+   (10 s error, the full first IERS ΔAT). Pinned in `prop_epoch_utc_roundtrip`.
+
+4. **`Epoch::from_str` panics on multi-byte UTF-8 input**
+   (`src/epoch/mod.rs:1131`): format sniffing does `&s[..2] == "JD"` /
+   `&s[..3] == "MJD"` after only checking `s.len() >= 7` in *bytes*. Any input
+   ≥7 bytes whose byte 2 (or 3) falls inside a multi-byte character panics with
+   "byte index 2 is not a char boundary". Minimal shrunk counterexample:
+   `"\u{0800}\u{10000}"` (2 chars, 7 bytes). Since `FromStr` returns a `Result`
+   and is fed by serde deserialization, this is a DoS-able panic on untrusted
+   input. Pinned in `prop_epoch_from_str_never_panics`.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Exact round-trips for dynamical time scales (ET/TDB/TCB)** — deliberately
+  not asserted. The docs describe the TDB/ET conversion as an iterated
+  approximation (convergence threshold 1e-9 s), so the property uses a 100 ns
+  tolerance instead (the crate's own `formal_epoch_reciprocity_tdb` tolerates
+  up to 0.5 ms at extreme epochs; 100 ns held over ±292 years at 10× cases).
+- **Epoch ordering consistency across time scales** — considered and dropped as
+  non-falsifiable: `Ord for Epoch` is *defined* as comparison of the TAI
+  conversion, so the test would restate the implementation.
+- **`Duration::Display` exact round-trip over the full range** — the property
+  itself is sound (serde relies on it) and is kept as a failing bug pin; the
+  originally hoped-for "holds everywhere" version is unattainable while
+  `decompose()` is f64-based, so the green sibling documents the sub-day domain
+  where exactness genuinely holds.
+- No other property had to be weakened: all remaining assertions are exact
+  (Duration oracle arithmetic, Gregorian and time-of-week round-trips,
+  fixed-offset time scale conversions).
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The boundary-boost advice paid off immediately: every one of the four bugs was
+  found by a boosted branch (`sampled_from` extremes, leap-second boundary
+  offsets) within the default 100 cases, and shrinking produced clean minimal
+  counterexamples (`ZERO - MIN`, a 7-byte 2-char string).
+- The "pin known failures deterministically via a `just` branch + KNOWN FAILURE
+  comment, then exclude the region from siblings" workflow from SKILL.md mapped
+  perfectly onto this crate; verified pins reproduce on fresh runs without the
+  failure database.
+- Friction: there is no global case-count override, so the exploratory 10× run
+  required sed-editing every attribute and reverting (as the reference warns).
+  A `HEGEL_TEST_CASES` env var would remove the most error-prone step.
+- Friction (minor): a failing hegel test in one integration test binary makes
+  bare `cargo test` stop before later binaries; `--no-fail-fast` is needed to
+  see the full picture once known-failure pins exist. Worth a note in the
+  reference next to the pinning section.
+- The Rust reference was accurate throughout — every generator/macro signature
+  compiled first try (`one_of!`, `#[hegel::composite]`, `sampled_from` with
+  owned enums, `just`, `from_regex().fullmatch(true)`).
